5. ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE, MACHINE LEARNING, RETRIEVAL, AND AUTOMATED EXTRACTION

5.1 CrownThrive reserves its rights in CrownThrive Material used for or exposed through AI, ML, LLM, retrieval, embedding, classification, recommendation, generation, evaluation, agent, automation, or search systems.

5.2 Unless separately authorized or independently permitted by applicable law, repository access does not grant permission to use CrownThrive Material for:

(a) commercial foundation-model or application-model training;
(b) fine-tuning, continued pretraining, distillation, teacher/student transfer, or reinforcement training;
(c) embedding or vector corpora intended to reproduce, substitute for, or commercially exploit protected CrownThrive knowledge;
(d) synthetic-data generation derived from protected CrownThrive material for training or commercial corpus expansion;
(e) competitive evaluation, benchmarking, red-teaming, or test corpora designed primarily to reproduce or reveal protected methods, proprietary taxonomies, prompts, scoring logic, or protected system behavior;
(f) automated bulk extraction, scraping, harvesting, crawling, mirroring, or database reconstruction beyond independently lawful or expressly authorized use;
(g) reconstruction of proprietary algorithms, prompt libraries, model policies, evaluation sets, knowledge graphs, economic logic, institutional decision systems, or trade-secret methods;
(h) creation of a substantially substitutive commercial knowledge product, clone, lookalike system, or competing institutional framework derived from CrownThrive Material.

5.3 This section does not purport to restrict rights that applicable law makes non-waivable. It also does not convert ordinary search indexing, accessibility processing, browser caching, or other independently lawful technical activity into a prohibited use merely because automated systems are involved.

5.4 Any separate AI/data license must identify at minimum the source corpus, versions, permitted models/systems, purpose, training status, retention, derivative-output rules, attribution if required, security, sublicensing, economics, audit, termination, deletion/cessation obligations, and restrictions on reconstruction of confidential methods.

